// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Mechanics describes how things move and why. Calculus makes it precise.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Velocity v = dx/dt and acceleration a = dv/dt. For constant acceleration: v = v₀ + at, x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at², v² = v₀² + 2aΔx. Projectiles: horizontal velocity is constant; vertical acceleration is −g (9.8 m/s²)."},{"t":"p","text":"Newton's laws: (1) inertia; (2) F_net = ma (vector sum of forces); (3) equal and opposite force pairs. Draw free-body diagrams."},{"t":"p","text":"Forces: weight mg, normal force, tension, friction (f_s ≤ μ_sN, f_k = μ_kN), springs F = −kx. Uniform circular motion: a = v²/r toward the center."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 5 kg box is pulled with 30 N on a surface with friction force 10 N. Find its acceleration.","steps":["F_net = 30 − 10 = 20 N","a = 20 ÷ 5"],"answer":"4 m/s²"}]},{"say":"Energy and momentum are conserved, which makes many hard problems simple.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Work W = ∫F·dx (= Fd cos θ for constant force). Kinetic energy K = ½mv²; the work–energy theorem: W_net = ΔK. Potential energy: gravitational U = mgh, spring U = ½kx². With only conservative forces, mechanical energy is conserved. Power P = dW/dt."},{"t":"p","text":"Momentum p = mv; impulse J = FΔt = Δp. In isolated systems total momentum is conserved. Elastic collisions also conserve kinetic energy; inelastic ones don't (perfectly inelastic: objects stick together)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 2 kg cart at 3 m/s hits and sticks to a 1 kg cart at rest. Find the final speed.","steps":["Momentum: 2 × 3 = (2 + 1)v"],"answer":"v = 2 m/s"}]}],[{"say":"Spinning objects follow rules parallel to straight-line motion.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Angular quantities: θ, ω = dθ/dt, α = dω/dt; v = rω. Torque τ = rF sin θ; τ_net = Iα, where moment of inertia I = Σmr² depends on mass distribution. Rotational KE = ½Iω². Angular momentum L = Iω is conserved without external torque (a skater pulling in arms spins faster)."},{"t":"p","text":"Gravitation: F = Gm₁m₂/r². Orbits: circular orbital speed v = √(GM/r); Kepler's third law T² ∝ r³. Gravitational potential energy U = −GMm/r."},{"t":"tip","text":"Every linear formula has a rotational twin: swap x for θ, v for ω, a for α, m for I, F for τ, and p for L."}]},{"say":"Springs, pendulums, sound and light all share the mathematics of oscillation.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Simple harmonic motion: x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ). For a mass on a spring ω = √(k/m), T = 2π√(m/k); for a small-angle pendulum T = 2π√(L/g). Energy sloshes between kinetic and potential."},{"t":"p","text":"Waves: v = fλ. Transverse versus longitudinal. Wave speed on a string v = √(T/μ). Superposition gives interference: constructive (in phase) and destructive. Standing waves on a string: λ = 2L/n. Doppler effect: approaching sources sound higher."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 4 m. Find its speed.","steps":["v = fλ = 50 × 4"],"answer":"200 m/s"}]}],[{"say":"Electric charges create fields, and moving charges make circuits work.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Coulomb's law F = kq₁q₂/r². Electric field E = F/q; potential V = U/q, with E = −dV/dx. Gauss's law relates flux to enclosed charge. Capacitance C = Q/V; energy stored ½CV²."},{"t":"p","text":"Circuits: current I = dQ/dt, Ohm's law V = IR, power P = IV = I²R. Series resistors add; parallel resistors combine as 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂. Kirchhoff's rules: the junction rule (charge conservation) and the loop rule (energy conservation). RC circuits charge with time constant τ = RC."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the equivalent resistance of 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel.","steps":["1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2"],"answer":"2 Ω"}]},{"say":"Magnetism and electricity are one force, and at small scales classical physics gives way to quantum rules.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Magnetic force on a charge F = qv × B and on a wire F = IL × B. Currents create magnetic fields (Ampère's law). Faraday's law: a changing magnetic flux induces an emf, ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz's law gives the direction opposing the change. This drives generators and transformers. Maxwell's equations unify E and B; light is an electromagnetic wave."},{"t":"p","text":"Modern physics: photon energy E = hf; the photoelectric effect shows light comes in quanta. Matter has wavelength λ = h/p. Atoms have discrete energy levels. Special relativity: time dilation, length contraction, E = mc². Nuclear decay follows half-lives."}]}]];
export default text;
